{
  "id": "site.st_michael",
  "name": "St Michael's Cistercian nunnery",
  "name_1343": "St Michael's (claustrum sanctimonialium Cisterciensis ordinis), the nuns' close on the west belt",
  "landmark_id": "landmark.st_michael",
  "confidence": "attested institution (founded 1249, patron of St Olaf since 1267); close extent, ranges, gate and garden plausible composite (no 1343 plan survives; close area unknown)",
  "sources": [
    "history/dossiers/religion/churches-and-religious-houses.md",
    "history/dossiers/religion/ecclesiastical-precinct-boundaries-1343.md"
  ],
  "period_notes": [
    "Church on the footprint of the surviving nunnery church (now the Transfiguration cathedral, Suur-Kloostri): a two-aisled vaulted hall with a short, narrower chancel and a flat east end (dossier I1 ~40-50 x 18-22 m; here 38 x 17.5 m).",
    "Cistercian restraint: plain gables, a ridge bell-cote instead of a tower, lime-washed interior, grisaille (colourless foliage) glass only, no murals or statues. The nuns hear mass from a west gallery (Nonnenempore) screened from the lay nave; lay people enter by the south portal from the street.",
    "Claustral ranges north of the church round a small cloister garth with timber lean-to walks (composite): west range (lay sisters, cellarer), north range (refectory, attested room 9.25 x 8.1 m in north-wing archaeology; kitchen at its west end), east range (chapter house, dormitory over).",
    "Close: a high limestone wall along the lanes with one controlled gate (porta) from the church square into the lay sisters' yard and service wing; kitchen-garden beds and orchard rows take most of the ground (dossier zone shares: built core 15-25%, garden/orchard 40-55%).",
    "Close area: unknown for 1343. The model fills the lane-bounded block west of Aida and south of Kooli (about 0.5 ha inside the wall, church included), keeping the street-front house rows; the curtain tie-in on the west/north (R-1720) is not modelled here.",
    "Not shown: the Nuns' Gate name (1355), the 1433 fire repairs, the post-Reformation school and Swedish-era fabric, the Orthodox cathedral fittings."
  ],
